{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>At the end of ten years</b> — this is the fixed deadline for a woman who has lived with her husband ten years and has not given birth: he is obligated to marry another." "<b>For Abram's dwelling in the land of Canaan</b> — this indicates that time spent outside the Land does not count toward the tally, because it was not said to him, 'and I will make of you a great nation,' until he came to the land of Israel — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 16:3). But this reason is not correct, for residence outside the Land is an established halakhah that does not count for anyone in the world. And the mishnah that we learned (Yevamot 64) applies to every person. And if it were on account of this promise to Abram, it would count for other people as well. Furthermore, some of the commentators also erred in it with another error, for they said that the law of one who dwells outside the Land does not apply to divorce the woman who lived with him ten years and did not give birth, nor [does it exempt him] from marrying another. But the matter is not so. Rather, the intent is to say that if a man was with his wife outside the Land five or ten years and they came to the land of Israel, they are given a period of ten years from the time of their entry into the Land — perhaps by the merit of the Land they will be built up. And so Abraham and Sarah did: they counted from the time they came there."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:29:56.423306967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}